// The attacker holds Alice's ciphertext in full — it travels unencrypted on
// the wire — and can hand it to Bob himself. Bob's checked decryption then
// succeeds, because it is the genuine message. That is a replay, not a
// forgery: Bob cannot tell it from the real delivery, and nothing the attacker
// did changed what he computed, so the authentication query must pass.
//
// Forging is out of reach in the other direction too. Producing any other
// AEAD_ENC under k requires k, which is shared privately and never travels;
// k is also the bypass key for the AEAD_DEC check, so a failed check genuinely
// halts Bob rather than being bypassed. There is exactly one value Bob will
// accept here and Alice is the only one who can produce it.
//
// This is the shape of scuttlebutt.vp's `authentication? Bob -> Alice:
// secretBox1Bob`, where a fully attacker-known ciphertext passes through a
// checked decryption whose key the attacker cannot reach. Verifpal 0.53
// reported an attack on that query on the strength of the value being
// "obtained by Attacker", with an attack trace containing no mutations at all.
// Being known is not the same as being forgeable, and this model pins the
// difference in isolation.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	knows private m
	generates n
	c = AEAD_ENC(k, m, n)
]

Alice -> Bob: c, n

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	m_b = AEAD_DEC(k, c, n)?
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: c
	confidentiality? m
]
